notes               = "Untitled in source. The father of father-fire and father-silences (146-147) returns as the one who forbade the music: پدرم گفت حرام است. خط خطی کردن is to scribble over, to cross out — set against کشیدن خطی, to draw one line, in the closing image, so the same word for line does both the crossing-out and the mark he never made. شصت سال here is the round figure, not a count (see muslin-memory, page 159). Bare year ۱۳۷۹ → +621 = 2000. Imported as a draft with machine / lantern layers; ===translation=== left for Ben."
draft               = true

===persian===

عاشق ساز آواز بودم
پدرم گفت حرام است.
شصت سال
آوازهای نخوانده‌ام را
خواب و بیدار
خط خطی کردم
بی آن که
خطی
به دیوار جهان
کشیده باشم...
تهران ۱۳۷۹

===machine===

I was in love with the instrument, with song.
My father said: it is forbidden.
For sixty years
the songs I never sang
sleeping and waking
I have scribbled over
without ever having drawn
one line
on the wall of the world...
Tehran 1379

===lantern===

I was in love with the instrument, with song.
My father said: it is forbidden.
For sixty years
I have scribbled over
the songs I never sang,
sleeping and waking,
without ever having drawn
one line
on the wall of the world...

Tehran 1379

===translation===

===footnotes===

The Persian sets خط خطی کردن — to scribble out, to cross through — against کشیدن خطی, to
draw a single line. The same word does the cancelling and the making, so sixty years of crossing
out amount to not having made one mark. English has to use two different words and loses the
knot.
